  [
    'id' => 60582,
    'slug' => 'zero-click-search-seo-measurement',
    'title' => 'Zero-Click Search: How to Measure SEO Success When Clicks Keep Falling',
    'category' => 'Search Engine Optimization',
    'tags' => 'Search Engine Optimization',
    'author' => 'Jithu Gilbert',
    'author_designation' => 'Senior Digital Strategist',
    'excerpt' => 'You open your monthly report and the traffic line is sloping down. Rankings haven’t moved much. Nobody has changed the website. Yet the number you’ve been told to care about keeps...',
    'body' => '<p>You open your monthly report and the traffic line is sloping down. Rankings haven’t moved much. Nobody has changed the website. Yet the number you’ve been told to care about keeps shrinking, and someone in the room asks the question: “Is our SEO still working?”</p><p>Maybe it isn’t. But before you answer, it helps to know that search itself has changed. Google now answers a growing share of questions without sending anyone anywhere. Here’s how to judge your results fairly when that happens.</p>			<h2>Zero-Click Search, Explained Simply
</h2>		<p>A zero-click search ends on the results page. The person types a question, reads the answer in a box, a map listing or an AI overview, and leaves satisfied. No website visit, so no click to count.</p><p>The numbers behind this are large.<a href="https://searchengineland.com/google-zero-click-searches-2026-study-479717"> SparkToro’s study of US Google searches</a> from January to April 2026 found that around 68% ended without a click. Keep in mind that this is US data, so Indian figures will differ, and the researchers themselves call AI Overviews a likely factor rather than a proven one. Still, the direction is hard to argue with.</p>			<h3>Where the Value Goes When the Click Disappears
</h3>		<p>The visit doesn’t vanish. It often turns into something else, just somewhere your analytics can’t see.</p><p>A customer reads your reviews on the map and phones you directly. Someone sees your name in an AI answer and searches for you by name later that week. A buyer taps the WhatsApp button on your Business Profile and never touches your site. Each of those is a real result, and none of them is a click.</p><p>That’s the core problem with a traffic-only report. It counts the one outcome that’s shrinking and ignores the ones that are growing.</p>			<h3>Five Numbers That Say More Than Traffic
</h3>		<h5><b>1. Impressions, Read Query by Query</b></h5><p>Impressions count how often your pages showed up, and clicks count visits, as<a href="https://support.google.com/webmasters/answer/7576553"> Google’s Performance report</a> explains. A sitewide impressions total can look fine while one valuable query quietly collapses. So compare two periods, sort the query list by change, and look at the biggest movers.</p><h5><b>2. Branded Searches</b></h5><p>When people search for your business by name, an earlier touchpoint did its job, whether that was a referral, a review or a<a href="https://techbound.in/brand-mentions/"> brand mention</a> somewhere else. In Search Console, filter queries with a regex like yourbrand|your brand and record the total monthly. A rising line is good news. A branded line that carries nearly everything is a warning that new customers aren’t discovering you.</p><h5><b>3. Calls, Directions and WhatsApp Chats</b></h5><p>For local and service businesses, this is the number that pays the bills. Google Business Profile reports calls, direction requests and website taps, and our write-up on<a href="https://techbound.in/google-business-profile-view-counts-and-review-responses/"> Google Business Profile updates</a> covers the return of post view counts. Tag your profile’s website link with UTM parameters, and keep a separate tally of WhatsApp enquiries.</p><h5><b>4. Conversion Rate and Lead Quality</b></h5><p>When fewer people arrive, each arrival should count for more. Doubling a page’s conversion rate has the same effect as doubling its traffic, and it’s usually cheaper. If your enquiry pages feel weak,<a href="https://techbound.in/website-no-leads-conversion-design-signs/"> these signs your website isn’t built to convert</a> are a good checklist. Beyond counting leads, track how many turned into real conversations.</p><h5><b>5. AI Mentions and Citations</b></h5><p>Pick 10 to 15 questions your customers actually ask. Once a month, put them into Google’s AI features and a couple of chat assistants, then note whether your business is named or your pages are cited. Search Console’s<a href="https://techbound.in/google-ai-overviews-report-search-console/"> AI Overviews reporting</a> offers a first signal, and our guide to<a href="https://techbound.in/ai-search-visibility-geo-guide-2026/"> checking if your business shows up in AI answers</a> walks through the manual check. The method is rough, but a rough picture is better than none.</p>			<h2>Reading the Signals Together
</h2>		<p>No single metric tells the whole story, so it helps to follow a simple path whenever clicks fall. Start with impressions, then work out whether the problem sits in visibility, citations, click-through, or somewhere off your website entirely.</p>													<img width="640" height="442" src="https://techbound.in/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/zero-click-search-measurement-flowchart-1024x707.png" alt="zero click search measurement flowchart" srcset="https://techbound.in/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/zero-click-search-measurement-flowchart-1024x707.png 1024w, https://techbound.in/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/zero-click-search-measurement-flowchart-300x207.png 300w, https://techbound.in/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/zero-click-search-measurement-flowchart-768x530.png 768w, https://techbound.in/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/zero-click-search-measurement-flowchart.png 1420w" sizes="(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 640px" />													<p> <i>A quick way to diagnose falling clicks before changing anything on your site.</i></p><p>Each ending needs a different fix. A citation problem calls for better-structured, more experience-led content. A visibility problem sends you back to rankings and technical checks. A click-through problem is usually about titles, snippets and what your page offers that a summary can’t. And when calls, chats and branded searches hold steady, the value has simply moved off your site, so report it next to clicks.</p>			<h2>Setting It Up in One Afternoon
</h2>		<p>You don’t need new software. Here’s a workable sequence:</p><ol><li style="font-weight: 400;" aria-level="1">In Search Console, save a comparison of the last three months against the previous period.</li><li style="font-weight: 400;" aria-level="1">Add a branded-query filter and note the monthly total.</li><li style="font-weight: 400;" aria-level="1">Pull Business Profile calls, directions and website taps into one sheet.</li><li style="font-weight: 400;" aria-level="1">Add a “How did you find us?” dropdown to your enquiry form, and log WhatsApp chats separately.</li><li style="font-weight: 400;" aria-level="1">Write down your 10 to 15 priority questions and check AI answers once a month.</li></ol><p>Put these five on one page. Traffic can still appear on it. It just shouldn’t open the report.</p>			<h2>Content That Still Earns the Click
</h2>		<p>Some searches will always send visitors: comparisons, local decisions, pricing questions and anything that needs a tool, template or opinion. Build for those. On informational pages, give readers something a quick summary can’t replace, such as a worked example, a checklist or experience from real projects. A named author and specific detail also strengthen E-E-A-T, which helps with both search results and AI citations.</p>			<h2>Frequently Asked Questions
</h2>		<h5><b>Does zero-click search mean SEO is dying?</b></h5><p>No. It means the click is one outcome among several. Visibility, brand recall and calls still come from good SEO.</p><h5><b>Should I stop tracking organic traffic?</b></h5><p>Keep it, but read it next to impressions, branded searches and conversions, not on its own.</p><h5><b>How can I tell if an AI Overview is behind a drop?</b></h5><p>Find queries where impressions fell sharply while average position stayed similar. Search them yourself and see whether an AI answer now appears.</p><h5><b>Which metric matters most for a local business?</b></h5><p>Calls and direction requests from your Business Profile, checked against how many enquiries became customers.</p>			<h2>The Bottom Line
</h2>		<p>A falling traffic line is a prompt to look closer, not proof that something broke. Measure visibility, demand, action and lead quality together, and you’ll know whether your SEO is working even on the days Google keeps the click.</p><p>If you’d like help building a report along these lines, our team at Techbound, a<a href="https://techbound.in/"> digital marketing company in Trivandrum</a>, is happy to talk it through.</p><p><br /><br /></p>',
    'image' => 'https://techbound.in/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Zero-Click-SEO-Success-Dashboard-1.png',
    'published_at' => '2026-10-22 15:08:20',
    'active' => 1,
  ],
  [
    'id' => 60570,
    'slug' => 'influencer-marketing-guide',
    'title' => 'Influencer Marketing: How Brands Turn Creator Trust into Real Business Results',
    'category' => 'Social Media Marketing',
    'tags' => 'Social Media Marketing',
    'author' => 'Jithu Gilbert',
    'author_designation' => 'Senior Digital Strategist',
    'excerpt' => 'Think back to the last thing you bought because someone online recommended it. It probably wasn’t a banner ad that did the convincing. It was a person you follow, mentioning...',
    'body' => '<p>Think back to the last thing you bought because someone online recommended it. It probably wasn’t a banner ad that did the convincing. It was a person you follow, mentioning almost casually that a product had worked for them.</p><p>That small moment is the whole idea behind influencer marketing. It’s also why it’s so easy to get wrong. Brands hear “creators work” and rush in, then wonder why a pile of posts produced nothing. So here’s how it actually works, which creators fit which goals, and how to measure a campaign without fooling yourself.</p>			<h2>What Is Influencer Marketing?
</h2>		<p>Influencer marketing is a form of social media marketing where a brand partners with a creator to promote a product, service or message to that creator’s audience. The creator tells it in their own voice, on their own channel, and people listen because they already trust them.</p><p>Here’s what sets it apart from a regular ad: who’s talking. In an ad, the brand speaks about itself. In a collaboration, someone the audience chose to follow speaks for you. That trust is the real thing you’re paying for, which is why the wrong creator can cost you money even when the post looks great. If you’re still getting comfortable with the wider channel, our<a href="https://techbound.in/social-media-marketing-guide-2026/"> social media marketing guide</a> shows where creator content fits alongside organic and paid posts.</p>			<h2>Types of Influencers and When to Use Each
</h2>		<p>Creators are usually sorted by follower count. Fair enough, but we’ve found the quality of the audience tells you more than the size of it.</p>			<h3>Nano and Micro Creators
</h3>		<p>Nano creators typically have under 10,000 followers, and micro creators sit between 10,000 and 100,000. Their followers often know them personally, or follow them for one narrow interest, so a recommendation feels like advice from a friend. If you run a restaurant, clinic, boutique or coaching centre in one city, this is usually the most cost-effective place to start.</p>			<h3>Macro Creators and Celebrities
</h3>		<p>These creators reach hundreds of thousands, sometimes millions. They make sense for launches and big awareness pushes. The catch is higher fees and thinner engagement per follower, so it’s harder to say which sale came from which post.</p>			<h3>Subject Experts
</h3>		<p>A doctor, architect, chartered accountant or trainer with a loyal following can carry more weight than a general lifestyle creator. When trust is the biggest hurdle to buying, an expert’s opinion often does more than raw reach ever could.</p>			<h2>How to Plan an Influencer Marketing Campaign
</h2>		<p>A good plan doesn’t have to be complicated. It just has to be specific.</p>													<img width="640" height="260" src="https://techbound.in/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/influencer-marketing-campaign-flow-1024x416.png" alt="influencer marketing campaign flow" srcset="https://techbound.in/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/influencer-marketing-campaign-flow-1024x416.png 1024w, https://techbound.in/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/influencer-marketing-campaign-flow-300x122.png 300w, https://techbound.in/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/influencer-marketing-campaign-flow-768x312.png 768w, https://techbound.in/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/influencer-marketing-campaign-flow-1536x624.png 1536w, https://techbound.in/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/influencer-marketing-campaign-flow.png 1600w" sizes="(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 640px" />																<h3>Step 1: Pick One Goal
</h3>		<p>Is the campaign for awareness, leads, sales or content? Choose one. That single decision shapes which creator you approach, what you ask them to make and which numbers you’ll look at afterwards. Campaigns that chase all four usually end up proving none.</p>			<h3>Step 2: Vet the Creator Properly
</h3>		<p>Don’t stop at the profile. Open the comments and actually read them. Are people having real conversations, or is it a wall of fire emojis? Check where the audience lives, whether the creator promoted a competitor last month, and whether engagement holds steady or jumps around. A creator with 15,000 relevant followers can easily beat one with 300,000 scattered ones.</p>			<h3>Step 3: Choose the Right Format
</h3>		<p>You have plenty of options: sponsored reels, product seeding, honest reviews, event coverage, affiliate links or a long-term ambassador deal. For a first campaign, a small paid collaboration or a commission-based arrangement keeps the risk low while you find out what works.</p>			<h3>Step 4: Brief, Do Not Script
</h3>		<p>Share the goal, the main message, any facts that must be accurate and the legal basics. Then step back. Audiences can spot a read-out script within seconds, and they scroll straight past it.</p>			<h3>Step 5: Disclose Clearly
</h3>		<p>Disclosure protects the brand and the creator. In India, the Department of Consumer Affairs’<a href="https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleasePage.aspx?PRID=1892527"> Endorsement Know-Hows guidelines</a> ask influencers to label paid or barter promotions in simple, clear terms such as “advertisement”, “sponsored”, “collaboration” or “paid promotion”. A visible label works. A tag buried under twenty hashtags doesn’t.</p>			<h2>How to Measure Influencer Marketing Results
</h2>		<p>Likes are easy to count and just as easy to misread. Go back to the goal you set in step one and track what matches it.</p><ul><li style="font-weight: 400;" aria-level="1"><b>Awareness:</b> reach, impressions, video views and profile visits</li><li style="font-weight: 400;" aria-level="1"><b>Engagement:</b> saves, shares, comments and average watch time</li><li style="font-weight: 400;" aria-level="1"><b>Traffic:</b> clicks tracked through<a href="https://support.google.com/analytics/answer/10917952"> UTM links</a> in Google Analytics, read alongside your wider<a href="https://techbound.in/how-to-increase-website-traffic-2026/"> website traffic</a> numbers</li><li style="font-weight: 400;" aria-level="1"><b>Conversions:</b> unique discount codes, dedicated landing pages or enquiry form submissions</li></ul><p>One small habit pays off every time. Once the post is live, ask the creator for screenshots of their native analytics. Saves, shares and audience location say far more than a public like count.</p>			<h2>Influencer Marketing and Search Visibility
</h2>		<p>Creator content does more than bring social reach. When a creator says your brand name in a video, caption or review, those<a href="https://techbound.in/brand-mentions/"> brand mentions</a> make people more likely to remember you and search for you by name later. And because more people now look up reviews and demos on social platforms before they open a search engine, creator content also feeds into<a href="https://techbound.in/blog-social-search-strategy-2026/"> social search</a>.</p>			<h2>Common Influencer Marketing Mistakes
</h2>		<p>A few patterns show up again and again when campaigns underperform:</p><ul><li style="font-weight: 400;" aria-level="1">Choosing creators purely by follower count</li><li style="font-weight: 400;" aria-level="1">Expecting a single post to trigger an overnight sales spike</li><li style="font-weight: 400;" aria-level="1">Skipping a written agreement on deliverables, deadlines and content usage rights</li><li style="font-weight: 400;" aria-level="1">Ignoring audience location, so a campaign for a Trivandrum shop ends up in front of viewers in another country</li></ul>			<h2>Frequently Asked Questions
</h2>		<h5><b>What is influencer marketing in simple words?</b></h5><p>It’s when a brand partners with a trusted creator so that the creator’s followers hear about a product or service through posts, reels, videos or stories.</p><h5><b>How much does influencer marketing cost?</b></h5><p>It depends on the creator’s reach, platform, content format and usage rights. Nano and micro creators are usually far more affordable than celebrities, and some are happy to work on product exchange or commission.</p><h5><b>Is influencer marketing suitable for small businesses?</b></h5><p>Yes, as long as the creator’s audience matches your customers. Local businesses often get the best results from micro creators in their own city or industry.</p><h5><b>Which platform is best for influencer marketing?</b></h5><p>Instagram and YouTube suit most consumer brands, while LinkedIn works better for B2B. The right platform is simply wherever your buyers already spend their time.</p><h5><b>How can I spot fake followers?</b></h5><p>Watch for sudden follower jumps, repetitive comments, very low engagement compared with audience size, and followers concentrated in countries unrelated to the creator’s market.</p>			<h2>Planning Your First Campaign
</h2>		<p>Influencer marketing rewards brands that stay patient, specific and honest. Choose the right creators, set one clear goal, disclose properly and measure what matters. If you’d like help planning social campaigns that connect to real enquiries, our team at Techbound, a<a href="https://techbound.in/"> digital marketing agency in Trivandrum</a>, is happy to help.</p>',
    'image' => 'https://techbound.in/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Influencer-Marketing_-Creator-Trust-in-Action-1.png',
    'published_at' => '2026-10-19 15:43:39',
    'active' => 1,
  ],
  [
    'id' => 60552,
    'slug' => 'social-commerce-scroll-led-discovery',
    'title' => 'Social Commerce and Scroll-Led Discovery: How People Buy Now',
    'category' => 'Social Media Marketing',
    'tags' => 'Social Media Marketing',
    'author' => 'Jithu Gilbert',
    'author_designation' => 'Senior Digital Strategist',
    'excerpt' => 'Think about the last product you bought after seeing it online. You probably didn’t search for it first. Instead, a short video caught your eye while you were scrolling, and a few...',
    'body' => '<p>Think about the last product you bought after seeing it online. You probably didn’t search for it first. Instead, a short video caught your eye while you were scrolling, and a few taps later you were asking the seller a question. That is social commerce in action, and it is changing where buying decisions begin.</p><p>People still search, of course. However, more of them now discover products while scrolling, long before they know what to search for. This habit is called scroll-led discovery, and it matters for every business that sells online or through chat.</p>			<h2>What Scroll-Led Discovery Means
</h2>		<p>The old path was simple. A person had a need, searched for it, compared a few options, and bought. As a result, the business that appeared at the right moment won.</p><p>Scroll-led discovery flips that order. The person isn’t looking for anything yet. Then a Reel, a creator recommendation, or a product demo appears in the feed and sparks interest. Only after that does the buying journey start.</p><p>This doesn’t replace search. It works alongside it. In fact, we explored how people now search inside social apps in our guide to<a href="https://techbound.in/blog-social-search-strategy-2026/"> social search strategy</a>, and the two behaviours often feed each other.</p>			<h2>Why Social Commerce Took Off in the Feed
</h2>		<p>A product page can describe an item. A short video, on the other hand, can show it in use, in a real room, in real hands. That difference matters most for products people want to see before they trust them.</p><p>Trust is the second reason. A recommendation from a real person feels different from an advert. Among the businesses we work with, more enquiries now begin with “I saw this on Instagram” than with “I searched for this.” The sale still has to be earned, but the first impression now happens in the feed.</p>			<h2>Short-Form Video Does the Showing
</h2>		<h5><b>Show the Product in Use</b></h5><p>The videos that work are usually plain and practical. How does the product look in daily life? Which problem does it solve? What would a buyer ask before ordering? Often, a short and honest answer on camera beats a polished advert. Our<a href="https://techbound.in/social-media-marketing-guide-2026/"> social media marketing guide</a> explains how these formats fit into a wider plan.</p><h5><b>Work With Creators Who Fit Your Audience</b></h5><p>Creator partnerships work best when the audience and the product truly match. For example, a smaller creator with a loyal local following can bring more relevant attention than a famous name with a scattered audience. Also, label paid partnerships clearly. This protects both the creator’s credibility and your brand.</p><h5><b>Conversational Messaging Does the Closing</b></h5><p>Video builds interest. A conversation usually closes the sale. Buyers want to know the price, the size, the delivery time, or whether an item is in stock. Most would rather ask in a chat than fill in a long form.</p><p>That is why click-to-message ads and WhatsApp commerce have become so popular in social commerce. Instead of sending people to a website, the ad opens a direct chat with the business. Meta explains the setup in its guide to<a href="https://www.facebook.com/business/help/447934475640650"> creating ads that click to WhatsApp</a>.</p><p>The tools are also easier than many owners expect. The free<a href="https://business.whatsapp.com/"> WhatsApp Business</a> app offers a product catalogue, quick replies, greeting messages, and labels for organising chats. For many small businesses, that is enough to start.</p>			<h3>A Simple Social Commerce Path for a Small Business
</h3>		<p>You don’t need a complex funnel. A clear, repeatable path usually works well:</p><ol><li style="font-weight: 400;" aria-level="1">A short video or ad shows the product and invites a question.</li><li style="font-weight: 400;" aria-level="1">A tap opens a chat with a friendly greeting and answers to common questions.</li><li style="font-weight: 400;" aria-level="1">The catalogue, price, and delivery details follow.</li><li style="font-weight: 400;" aria-level="1">The order is confirmed in the same chat, and a follow-up message checks in afterwards.</li></ol><p>Step two is the weak point in most accounts we review. Good content attracts the message, but a slow reply loses it. Therefore, speed and clarity in the chat are often worth more than another round of creative.</p>			<h3>Measure What Actually Matters
</h3>		<p>Social commerce results are easy to misread. Views feel good, but they don’t pay the bills. Instead, track how many conversations start, how many become real enquiries, and how many end in orders. Also, ask new customers how they found you. Their honest answer often points straight to a specific video.</p>			<h2>Mistakes That Quietly Cost Sales
</h2>		<p>Four social commerce patterns come up again and again:</p><ul><li style="font-weight: 400;" aria-level="1">Treating the chat like a basic inbox instead of a sales conversation</li><li style="font-weight: 400;" aria-level="1">Replying hours later, after the buyer’s interest has cooled</li><li style="font-weight: 400;" aria-level="1">Posting attractive content without clear price and delivery details</li><li style="font-weight: 400;" aria-level="1">Forgetting that buyers still check you elsewhere</li></ul><p>That last point deserves attention. Someone who finds you in a feed will often look you up before buying, on search, on maps, or in an AI answer. So your website and profiles should tell the same story as your social content. We cover this in our guide to<a href="https://techbound.in/ai-search-visibility-geo-guide-2026/"> AI search visibility</a>.</p>			<h3>Where Social Commerce Fits in Your Marketing
</h3>		<p>Social commerce works best as part of a connected system. Video creates the interest, messaging closes the sale, and search plus your online profiles give people the reassurance to go ahead. When these pieces support each other, the journey feels smoother for the buyer.</p><p>If you’d like help building that system, the team at a<a href="https://techbound.in"> digital marketing agency in Trivandrum</a> is always happy to talk it through.</p>			<h2>Frequently Asked Questions
</h2>		<h5><b>What is scroll-led discovery?</b></h5><p>It is the way people find products by scrolling through social feeds and short videos instead of starting with a search. Interest comes first, and the purchase follows.</p><h5><b>Is social commerce only for online stores?</b></h5><p>No. Service businesses and local shops use it too. Anyone who can show their work and answer questions in a chat can benefit, from salons and clinics to builders and home-service providers.</p><h5><b>Do I need the WhatsApp Business API to start?</b></h5><p>Not necessarily. Many small businesses begin with the free WhatsApp Business app. However, the API becomes useful later, when message volume grows and you need automation or detailed reporting.</p><h5><b>How long does social commerce take to show results?</b></h5><p>It varies. Still, steady posting and fast, helpful replies usually matter more than any single video. Give a consistent routine a few weeks before judging it, and track conversations instead of views.</p>',
    'image' => 'https://techbound.in/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Social-Commerce-and-Scroll-Led-Discovery.png',
    'published_at' => '2026-10-14 16:33:53',
    'active' => 1,
  ],
  [
    'id' => 60538,
    'slug' => 'chatgpt-visual-ads-measurement',
    'title' => 'ChatGPT Ads Are Going Visual: What Advertisers Should Prepare For',
    'category' => 'AI & Emerging Tech',
    'tags' => 'AI & Emerging Tech',
    'author' => 'Jithu Gilbert',
    'author_designation' => 'Senior Digital Strategist',
    'excerpt' => 'A few weeks ago, ChatGPT ads were a small labelled card under a text answer. Simple to understand, and easy to ignore if you were busy. This month the conversation moved on....',
    'body' => '<p>A few weeks ago, ChatGPT ads were a small labelled card under a text answer. Simple to understand, and easy to ignore if you were busy. This month the conversation moved on. OpenAI has announced a visual ad format and a stronger set of measurement tools, and together they say a lot about where the platform is heading.</p><p>If you read our earlier note on<a href="https://techbound.in/chatgpt-ads-india-should-you-advertise/"> ChatGPT ads in India</a>, you already know how the basic format works. This post is about what comes next, and what sensible advertisers in India should do about it right now, which is less than the headlines suggest.</p>			<h2>What OpenAI Announced on October 5
</h2>		<p>OpenAI said it will begin testing visual ads during image generation in ChatGPT, starting later this month in the US. According to<a href="https://bleepingcomputer.com/news/artificial-intelligence/openai-will-show-visual-ads-in-chatgpt-while-you-generate-images"> BleepingComputer</a>, the ads may use imagery to show product inspiration, how a product is used, or the experience tied to a service. OpenAI says the ads will be clearly labelled and kept separate from the image being created, and it repeated that ads do not influence ChatGPT’s answers.</p><p>Two things are worth noticing. The test is small and limited to the US, and OpenAI has not shared when wider availability might come. Nothing has been confirmed for India. So this is a signal about direction, not something you can buy tomorrow.</p>													<img width="640" height="371" src="https://techbound.in/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/chatgpt-ad-formats-comparison-1024x594.png" alt="chatgpt ad formats comparison" srcset="https://techbound.in/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/chatgpt-ad-formats-comparison-1024x594.png 1024w, https://techbound.in/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/chatgpt-ad-formats-comparison-300x174.png 300w, https://techbound.in/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/chatgpt-ad-formats-comparison-768x445.png 768w, https://techbound.in/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/chatgpt-ad-formats-comparison-1536x891.png 1536w, https://techbound.in/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/chatgpt-ad-formats-comparison.png 1647w" sizes="(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 640px" />																<h2>Why Image Generation, of All Places?
</h2>		<p>Our read, as people who spend most days inside ad accounts, is that it is a smart starting point. Someone asking for a mock-up of a living room, a wedding invitation theme, or a product concept is usually mid-decision. They are already imagining an outcome. A relevant visual at that moment can feel like a suggestion rather than an interruption, provided it stays clearly separate from what the user is creating. If that line blurs, trust drops quickly, and OpenAI seems aware of it.</p>													<img width="640" height="371" src="https://techbound.in/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/chatgpt-visual-ad-example-illustration-1024x594.png" alt="chatgpt visual ad example illustration" srcset="https://techbound.in/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/chatgpt-visual-ad-example-illustration-1024x594.png 1024w, https://techbound.in/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/chatgpt-visual-ad-example-illustration-300x174.png 300w, https://techbound.in/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/chatgpt-visual-ad-example-illustration-768x445.png 768w, https://techbound.in/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/chatgpt-visual-ad-example-illustration-1536x891.png 1536w, https://techbound.in/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/chatgpt-visual-ad-example-illustration.png 1647w" sizes="(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 640px" />													<p>“Illustration only. The real format may look different when it launches.”</p>			<h2>What Changes for Creative Teams
</h2>		<p>Text-only placements rewarded a sharp line and a clear offer. Visual placements will reward real imagery: finished projects, products in use, honest before-and-after shots. Businesses with a decent photo library will move faster than those who need to build one from nothing. Generic stock photos will probably look out of place in a format built around showing how something is actually used.</p>			<h2>Measurement and Brand Suitability: The Quieter Part of the Update
</h2>		<p>The visual format got the headlines, but we think the measurement side matters more for anyone spending real money. OpenAI is adding tools that help advertisers see whether ads lead to purchases or other conversions, and it is working with DoubleVerify and Integral Ad Science on brand suitability.</p><p>That second point deserves a moment. On a normal website you can exclude pages you do not want to appear beside. In a chat, the “page” is a conversation nobody can predict. Third-party verification is a way of giving advertisers some confidence about where their brand lands. For agencies, it also means monthly reports can rest on conversion data rather than clicks and impressions alone, which makes client conversations far more honest.</p>			<h3>Conversational Ads: What Has Been Reported So Far
</h3>		<p>There is a third thread to watch. As<a href="https://thenextweb.com/news/openai-is-hiring-ad-tech-firms-to-make-chatgpt-ads-talk-back-to-you"> The Next Web reported</a>, OpenAI has been working with ad-tech firm Smartly on interactive formats, where a user clicks an ad and enters a tailored chat experience, almost a small conversation inside the larger one. This comes from early partnership reporting, so treat the specifics as likely to change.</p><p>If it does arrive, the way we judge performance will shift. Clicks will matter less than whether the conversation actually helped someone decide. Nobody has settled on standard metrics for that yet, so early advertisers will be writing their own rulebook.</p>			<h2>What This Means for Businesses in India
</h2>		<h4><b>Treat Visual Ads as a Preview, Not a To-Do Item</b></h4><p>The visual test is US-only for now. Businesses using the self-serve platform that opened in India last month should keep running what is available and watch for announcements. Rushing to build campaigns for a format you cannot yet buy helps nobody.</p><h4><b>Preparation That Costs Almost Nothing</b></h4><p>Most of the useful preparation has little to do with media budgets.</p><p>First, organise your visual assets: real project photos, product shots, and short clips of the service at work. Second, fix your landing pages. If a ChatGPT user clicks through and meets a slow, cluttered mobile page, no ad format will save that visit. Third, set up conversion tracking properly, because a platform leaning toward measurement rewards advertisers who can connect a click to an enquiry or a call. Fourth, watch your organic footprint. Paid placement and<a href="https://techbound.in/ai-search-visibility-geo-guide-2026/"> AI search visibility</a> feed each other, and a brand that AI tools understand clearly is easier to advertise as well.</p><h4><b>Where It Fits in a Local Marketing Mix</b></h4><p>For businesses whose customers are local and influenced offline, such as renovation contractors, gold buyers, or property sellers, we still see Google Business Profile, local SEO, and Meta as the core. ChatGPT is a small test channel. Our guide to<a href="https://techbound.in/our-services/online-advertising-agency-in-kerala/"> online advertising in Kerala</a> explains how we usually split budgets, and our earlier post on the<a href="https://techbound.in/openai-chatgpt-ads-launch/"> ChatGPT ads launch</a> covers how this all began.</p>													<img width="640" height="428" src="https://techbound.in/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/chatgpt-ads-decision-flowchart-1024x684.png" alt="" srcset="https://techbound.in/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/chatgpt-ads-decision-flowchart-1024x684.png 1024w, https://techbound.in/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/chatgpt-ads-decision-flowchart-300x200.png 300w, https://techbound.in/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/chatgpt-ads-decision-flowchart-768x513.png 768w, https://techbound.in/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/chatgpt-ads-decision-flowchart-1536x1026.png 1536w, https://techbound.in/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/chatgpt-ads-decision-flowchart.png 1647w" sizes="(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 640px" />																<h2>What Could Go Wrong
</h2>		<p>It is fair to stay a little sceptical. Early formats on any new platform tend to change, and results from one market do not always carry over to another. Users may also react badly if ads feel too close to the creative work they are doing. OpenAI’s<a href="https://help-lb.openai.com/en/articles/20001207-ads-in-chatgpt-the-basics"> help documentation on ads in ChatGPT</a> is worth bookmarking, since policies on placement and sensitive topics can be updated. A small, tracked test is far wiser than a big bet.</p>			<h2>Frequently Asked Questions
</h2>		<h5><b>Are ChatGPT visual ads available in India?</b></h5><p>Not yet. OpenAI plans a US test with select advertisers later this month, and wider availability has not been announced.</p><h5><b>Will visual ads change what ChatGPT generates?</b></h5><p>OpenAI says the ads are separate from the generated image and do not influence responses.</p><h5><b>Does ChatGPT now offer conversion tracking?</b></h5><p>OpenAI says it is adding tools to measure purchases and other conversions. Exact features may change as the rollout continues.</p><h5><b>Should small businesses wait for visual ads before testing?</b></h5><p>Not necessarily. The current format can be tested at a modest budget while you prepare creative assets for later formats.</p>			<h2>Where to Start
</h2>		<p>New platforms change quickly, and the useful question is always where a channel fits your customers, not whether it is trending. A<a href="https://techbound.in/"> digital marketing agency in Trivandrum</a> like Techbound can help you review your current channels and decide when a ChatGPT test actually makes sense.</p>',
    'image' => 'https://techbound.in/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/chatgpt-ads-going-visual-featured-image.png',
    'published_at' => '2026-10-12 16:02:16',
    'active' => 1,
  ],
  [
    'id' => 60526,
    'slug' => 'seo-strategy-technical-seo-updates-october-2026',
    'title' => 'SEO Strategy and Technical SEO in October 2026: What Changed and What to Do Next',
    'category' => 'Search Engine Optimization',
    'tags' => 'Search Engine Optimization',
    'author' => 'Jithu Gilbert',
    'author_designation' => 'Senior Digital Strategist',
    'excerpt' => 'If your rankings have felt jumpy since September, you are not imagining it. In about six weeks, Google rolled out a spam update, refreshed several technical documents and finished...',
    'body' => '<p>If your rankings have felt jumpy since September, you are not imagining it. In about six weeks, Google rolled out a spam update, refreshed several technical documents and finished a worldwide release of its AI reports in Search Console. That is a lot for any site owner to keep up with.</p><p>We went through the latest announcements and documentation changes to separate what needs action from what is just noise. Here is what really matters for your SEO strategy and technical SEO this month.</p>			<h2>The September 2026 Spam Update Is Still the Main Story
</h2>		<p>Google\'s September 2026 spam update began in late September and, according to Search Engine Roundtable, was still moving through its phases in the first week of October. Spam updates go after manipulative tactics, so thin pages, scaled content and unnatural link patterns usually feel it first. We covered the early signals in our<a href="https://techbound.in/google-september-2026-spam-update/"> Google September 2026 spam update</a> breakdown, and the advice still holds: let the rollout settle before you judge any traffic movement.</p><p>Recent comments from Google\'s John Mueller add some useful context. He warned that programmatic SEO, meaning large sets of pages generated from templates and data lists, often ends up as spam, borderline spam or low-quality content. He also pointed out that once Google\'s systems lose confidence in a site, winning that trust back takes time and real effort.</p>			<h2>What to Check While the Update Settles
</h2>		<p>Look at Search Console by page type, not just across the whole site. Watch for template-based pages with very little unique value, near-identical location pages and old low-quality links pointing to your domain. Fix, merge or remove whatever is weak. Adding more pages on top of a shaky foundation rarely helps.</p>			<h2>Technical SEO Updates Worth Knowing
</h2>					<h3>Crawl Rate Documentation Now Explains Retry-After
</h3>		<p>On October 6, Google reorganised its documentation on reducing the crawl rate. The main addition is a clearer explanation of the Retry-After HTTP header. When a server returns a 503 or 429 status during an emergency, it can now tell Google\'s crawlers when to come back, either as a delay in seconds or as an exact UTC date and time. Search Engine Roundtable notes that the underlying mechanism has not changed, because those status codes already told Google to slow down.</p><p>In practice, this matters if you are on shared hosting or planning a migration or server maintenance. A temporary 503 with a sensible Retry-After is far safer than letting pages time out. Google\'s<a href="https://developers.google.com/crawling/docs/crawlers-fetchers/reduce-crawl-rate"> crawl rate guidance</a> has long warned that leaving error responses in place for too long can make URLs drop out of the index, so think of it as a short-term tool only.</p>			<h3>Page Experience and Structured Data
</h3>		<p>Google also added ad-related metrics to the Chrome User Experience Report and mentioned them in its page experience documentation. Google has suggested these are not a ranking factor, at least for now. Even so, it is a fair nudge to check whether intrusive ads or layout shifts are annoying real visitors. On the markup side, Google updated its video structured data guidance with a creator property, which is worth a look if you publish video.</p>			<h3>Search Console Now Shows How You Appear in AI Features
</h3>		<p>As of August 31, 2026, Google\'s generative AI performance report and AI controls are available to sites worldwide. The report sits as an expandable section under the main Performance report and shows impressions, pages, countries, devices and dates for AI features across Search and Discover. It does not show clicks or queries, and sites without enough data may not see it yet. Google\'s own post on the<a href="https://developers.google.com/search/blog/2026/06/gen-ai-performance-reports?hl=en"> generative AI performance reports</a> explains the rollout in detail.</p><p>There is also a toggle that lets site owners opt out of their content appearing in or around AI features. For most local and service businesses, visibility is the whole point, so opting out rarely makes sense. Publishers with licensed content may see it differently.</p><p>Google has also added a multimodal search type filter and multi-select country filters to the Performance report. If you serve more than one market, such as India and the Gulf, comparing regions side by side is now much easier. To go further on this front, read our guide on<a href="https://techbound.in/ai-search-visibility-geo-guide-2026/"> how to check if your business shows up in AI search answers</a>.</p>			<h3>What Google\'s Documentation Says About Strategy
</h3>		<p>Google\'s documentation changes this year all point the same way. The updated guidance stresses non-commodity content, meaning pages that offer something readers cannot find in a dozen near-identical articles. It also says that long-standing SEO best practices still support visibility in generative AI features. On October 1, Google refreshed its guide on using generative AI content to match the Search Quality Rater Guidelines.</p><p>That fits what we see in audits. AI-assisted drafting is fine, but unedited, mass-produced output with no original experience behind it is what gets filtered out. If you are trying to work out where SEO, AEO and GEO overlap, our post on<a href="https://techbound.in/ai-search-optimization-seo-aeo-geo/"> how SEO, AEO and GEO work together</a> breaks it down simply.</p>			<h3>A Practical October Checklist
</h3>		<ol><li style="font-weight: 400;" aria-level="1">Hold off on sweeping changes until the spam update finishes, and note the rollout dates against your Search Console trends.</li><li style="font-weight: 400;" aria-level="1">Audit thin and template-driven pages, then improve, merge or remove them.</li><li style="font-weight: 400;" aria-level="1">Make sure maintenance windows and server errors return a proper 503, ideally with a Retry-After header.</li><li style="font-weight: 400;" aria-level="1">Open the generative AI report in Search Console and note which pages appear in AI features.</li><li style="font-weight: 400;" aria-level="1">Strengthen your content with first-hand examples, named authors and data only you own.</li></ol><p>Once the technical foundation is steady, the next step is growth. Our guide on<a href="https://techbound.in/how-to-increase-website-traffic-2026/"> how to increase website traffic in 2026</a> covers the practical side.</p>			<h2>Frequently Asked Questions
</h2>		<p><b>Does the Retry-After header affect rankings?</b><b><br /></b> No. It is a crawl signal that tells Google when to come back. It is not a ranking factor.</p><p><b>Is programmatic SEO banned?</b><b><br /></b> Not outright. The risk comes from mass-producing pages with little real value, which Google\'s systems can treat as low quality.</p><p><b>How long does it take to recover from a spam update?</b><b><br /></b> There is no fixed timeline. Google\'s own comments suggest recovery takes time and real effort, so focus on improving quality steadily.</p><p><b>Should I block AI features from using my content?</b><b><br /></b> For most businesses looking for customers, visibility matters more. Publishers with paid or licensed content may reasonably decide otherwise.</p><p><b>Does technical SEO still matter with AI search?</b><b><br /></b> Yes. Google\'s documentation says established SEO best practices continue to support visibility in its generative AI features, and a site that cannot be crawled cleanly cannot be cited.</p><p>Keeping up with every update is easier with a second pair of eyes. If you would like help turning these changes into a practical plan, speak with a<a href="https://techbound.in"> digital marketing agency in Trivandrum</a> that has been helping businesses since 2009.</p>',
    'image' => 'https://techbound.in/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/seo-strategy-technical-seo-october-2026-1.png',
    'published_at' => '2026-10-07 18:03:52',
    'active' => 1,
  ],
