    'excerpt' => 'A recen t leak of internal Google documents has shook the SEO community, offering a never given glimpse into the inner workings of the search giant\'s ranking algorithm. This...',
    'body' => 'A recen<a href="http://unprecedented">t leak of internal Google documents</a>has shook the&nbsp; SEO community, offering a never given glimpse into the inner workings of the search giant\'s ranking algorithm.

This revelation has significant implications for SEO professionals and marketers, as it exposes previously unknown or unconfirmed aspects of how Google ranks websites, potentially requiring a reevaluation and adjustment of existing SEO strategies.
<h2>What is Google Search Algorithm Leak</h2>
Google is the Centre point when it comes to finding stuff online? Well, some of their <a href="https://www.theverge.com/2024/5/29/24167407/google-search-algorithm-documents-leak-confirmation">secret documents got leaked</a> on Github, and it\'s kind of a big deal.

Especially since it has unveiled <a href="https://www.pc-tablet.co.in/google-data-leak-exposes-past-privacy-mishaps/25621/">past privacy mishaps</a> affecting its trust value.

These documents give us a much better idea of how Google decides which websites get to be at the top of the search results.

And guess what? It turns out that some of the things Google <i>said</i> they don\'t care about, like how often people click on your site or how long they stay there, might actually matter a lot.
<h5>Details Yet To Come</h5>
Now, Google is saying, "Hold it, this isn\'t the whole picture!"

But even so, this leak is a treasure of information for SEO developers– the people who make websites show up higher in search results.

Want to know more about what this all means for your website and how to make sure it\'s a hit with Google? Keep reading, because we are unveiling the secrets of Google\'s algorithm!
<h2>Key Surprises from Google Search Algorithm Leak</h2>
<h3><strong>1. Domain Authority</strong></h3>
Remember when Google said they don\'t care about your website\'s overall reputation? Well, the leaked documents suggest they might have been bluffing a bit.

It seems like they actually do track something called "siteAuthority," which sounds a lot like <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Domain_authority">Domain authority</a>.
<h3><strong>2. Click Data</strong></h3>
Google also said they don\'t use information from your Chrome browsing history to decide how websites rank. But surprise! The leaked documents hint that they might be using those clicks after all.
<h3><strong>3. Subdomains</strong></h3>
Google used to say subdomains were just part of the main website.

Now, it looks like they\'re treating them like separate entities. So, if you have a blog on a subdomain, it\'s like it has its own little reputation to build.
<h3><strong>4. The Sandbox</strong></h3>
Ever heard of the <a href="https://ahrefs.com/seo/glossary/google-sandbox">"Google Sandbox</a>"? It\'s this idea that new websites have a harder time ranking well.

Google denied it existed, but the leaked documents mention something called "hostAge," which makes it seem like the sandbox might be real.
<h3><strong>5. Whitelisting</strong></h3>
This one is a bit tempting. <a href="https://medium.com/@SarahDreamer/googles-secret-algorithm-exposed-via-leak-to-github-bbb43211cd78?responsesOpen=true&amp;sortBy=REVERSE_CHRON">Google might have a "whitelist" of websites that get special treatment in certain situations, like during elections or for certain topics</a>. That\'s definitely not something they\'ve been open about.
<h2>Google Search Algorithm Leak: Affected and Beneficiaries</h2>
<h4><strong>SEO and Digital Marketers</strong></h4>
Suddenly, the SEO pros are juggling between the ongoing strategies and leaked secrets to stay ahead of the curve
<h4><strong>Publishers and Content Creators</strong></h4>
Google has just shouted, "Quality content is the King" Now, everyone\'s&nbsp; polishing their articles and blog posts, hoping to avoid the dreaded "sandbox."
<h4><strong>E-commerce</strong></h4>
Online stores are rethinking their whole strategy, like, "Should we start giving discounts to particularly chrome users.
<h4><strong>News and Media</strong></h4>
